    "conversation_id": "a2fb282c-5b36-4608-9eab-fe426b62e5ea",
    "status": "SUCCESS",
    "response": "It is understandable that this outcome feels frustrating, especially when your quantifiable academic metrics (a completed PhD, more papers, more years of university teaching) appear stronger on paper. \n\nHowever, looking at this objectively within the context of academic hiring, here is how you should evaluate the situation and what conclusions you can reasonably draw:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Does an all-female panel indicate bias?\n**On its own, no.**\n\n* **Administrative reality:** Hiring panels are typically composed based on institutional roles (e.g., department head, search committee chair, teaching program director) or faculty availability, rather than an orchestrated demographic setup. Just as an all-male panel (which has historically been common in economics) does not inherently prove bias against female candidates, an all-female panel does not prove bias against male candidates.\n* **Institutional rules:** Many public universities do have formal policies regarding committee composition. Some mandate gender diversity on panels (e.g., requiring at least one man and one woman). If you are curious, you can check the university’s public HR or academic hiring handbook to see if their procedural guidelines were followed, but the demographic composition itself is not legal or administrative proof of discrimination.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Why the other candidate may have been chosen\nLecturer appointments—particularly in public universities—are evaluated differently than pure research or tenure-track appointments:\n\n* **Departmental needs and \"Industry Experience\":** The announcement explicitly highlighted her *“strong experience from industry.”* If the department is expanding practical, applied, or professional tracks (e.g., financial economics, consultancy prep, public policy), industry experience can often be prioritized over academic publication counts.\n* **Fit and teaching style:** For lecturer positions, the teaching demonstration, student engagement approach, and sample lectures during the interview carry enormous weight. The panel may have felt her specific teaching approach or background better aligned with the courses they urgently need covered.\n* **PhD status:** While having a completed PhD is an advantage, it is common in economics to hire candidates in the final year of their PhD (ABD—All But Dissertation) if completion is imminent and their job-talk/teaching demonstration is compelling.\n* **Subject-area match:** Subfields matter deeply. A department might choose a candidate whose specific focus areas complement existing faculty gaps over someone with more total publications in different subfields.\n\n---\n\n### 3. The two-week delay in written feedback\nA two-week silence is normal in university administration:\n* Search committees rarely write feedback independently; feedback usually has to be vetted or approved by HR and departmental leadership to ensure consistency and compliance.\n* University administrative machinery moves slowly. Two weeks does not indicate evasion or bad faith.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Should you make something of it?\n**Practically speaking, raising the panel's gender composition is unlikely to help and carries significant professional risk:**\n\n* **Lack of actionable evidence:** Alleging or implying gender bias based solely on the panel's gender and the successful candidate's gender will almost certainly be dismissed unless there is documented evidence of procedural violations or explicit discriminatory remarks.\n* **Professional reputation:** Economics and academic circles can be close-knit. Raising unsubstantiated claims can damage your standing and future opportunities with that institution and peer departments.\n\n---\n\n### Recommended Next Steps\n\n1. **Send a polite follow-up for feedback:** \n   Wait another few days (around the 3-week mark), then send a brief, cordial follow-up:\n   > *\"I know this is a busy period in the semester, but I wanted to gently follow up on my request for feedback regarding the lecturer position. I appreciated the opportunity to interview and would value any insights the panel can share to help strengthen my profile for future applications.\"*\n2. **Focus on criteria, not demographics:** \n   If you receive feedback or speak with the chair, ask constructive questions about what profile elements or teaching areas the department prioritized.\n3. **Recognize your strong standing:** \n   Making the final shortlist of three and completing two rounds of interviews proves that your credentials and presentation are competitive. In close final decisions, outcomes often come down to specific departmental needs or subjective fit rather than a flaw in your qualifications.\n",
